-- A case whose branches are processes is written with the interpretation inside the branches
-- (lx-sum, lx-if): a process is a function of its interpretation, and a case on a response that
-- is applied to the interpretation from outside is stuck until the response is known, which
-- keeps the handler it is applied to in the compiled code as a value, every command dispatched
-- through it at run time (archive/2026-11-11/D-os.md, sha256 71c9c2608447…, “Track D — the operating system as a typed world (Phase 3; needed by D5)”, “Found”, item 7 “`lib/proc.cub`”). With the application inside, each
-- branch is a process the specializer runs through the handler now
lx-sum : {l : Level} {S : Sig} {A B C : Type} → Sum A B → (A → Proc {l} S C) → (B → Proc {l} S C) → Proc {l} S C
lx-sum {l} {S} {A} {B} {C} s f g = λ R k h → case s of inl a → f a R k h; inr b → g b R k h
